<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sąjūdis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1988">1988</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Reform Movement of Lithuania</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sąjūdis, the Reform Movement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, was a political movement founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a> in June 1988 that led Lithuania's campaign for independence from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>. Headed by the musicologist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vytautas-landsbergis">Vytautas Landsbergis</a>, it won a majority in the elections to the Supreme Council in February 1990. On 11 March 1990 that council declared the restoration of Lithuanian independence, the first such declaration by a Soviet republic.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sajudis.jpg" alt="Sąjūdis" width="320" height="201" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lietuvos Persitvarkymo Sąjūdis · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sajudis1.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In the mid-1980s the leadership of the Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> hesitated to embrace <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-gorbachev">Mikhail Gorbachev</a>'s perestroika and glasnost. The death in 1987 of Petras Griškevičius, first secretary of the party, was followed only by the appointment of another rigid communist, Ringaudas Songaila. Encouraged by Gorbachev's rhetoric, by the strengthening of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solidarity-polish-trade-union">Solidarity</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and by the Pope and the U.S. Government, Baltic independence activists began to hold public demonstrations in Riga, Tallinn and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a>.</p><p>On 3 June 1988, at a meeting at the Lithuanian Academy of Sciences, communist and non-communist intellectuals formed the Sąjūdis Initiative Group to organise a movement supporting glasnost, democratisation and perestroika. It had 35 members, mostly artists, and 17 of them were party members. In October 1988 a founding conference in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a> elected a 35-member council, chaired by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vytautas-landsbergis">Vytautas Landsbergis</a>, a professor of musicology who was not a party member.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Sąjūdis used mass meetings. A gathering on 21 June 1988 was followed by others, one in Vingis Park in July with about 100,000 people and one on 23 August 1988 with about 250,000, protesting against the Molotov-Ribbentrop pact and its secret protocol. A samizdat paper, Sąjūdis News, appeared on 19 June 1988, and about 150 newspapers in all supported the movement. Its demands included the Lithuanian language as the official language, the truth about the Stalinist years, protection of the environment and a halt to a third reactor at the Ignalina nuclear power plant. Its candidates won 36 of the 40 districts they contested in elections to the Congress of People's Deputies.</p><p>In February 1989 Sąjūdis declared that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> had been forcibly annexed and that its goal was restoring independence. Lithuanian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> was proclaimed in May 1989. On 23 August 1989 a human chain of two million people, 600 kilometres long, linked Tallinn and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a>, which became known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-way">Baltic Way</a>. In December the Communist Party of Lithuania seceded from the Soviet party and gave up its monopoly on power. In February 1990 Sąjūdis and its supporters won 101 of 141 seats in the Supreme Council, which elected Landsbergis its chairman and declared the restoration of independence on 11 March 1990.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>About a month after independence, reform communists and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a> liberal intellectuals left Sąjūdis, partly over growing nationalist rhetoric. Some formed the Independence Party, and liberal members helped found the Liberal Union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>. The movement lost support in rural areas over its agricultural reforms, and in 1992 its coalition lost the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seimas">Seimas</a> elections to the Democratic Labour Party, the former Communist Party. Many members, including Landsbergis, formed the core of the Homeland Union.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sąjūdis” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under Mikhail Gorbachev’s reforms the reform movement <b>Sąjūdis</b> was founded in 1988, and in February 1990 it won elections to the Supreme Council, which under Vytautas Landsbergis declared the restoration of independence on 11 March 1990.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-lithuania">Republic of Lithuania · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1988 the <b>Sąjūdis</b> reform movement emerged, and in 1989 the Lithuanian Communist Party under Algirdas Brazauskas broke from Moscow.</blockquote><a href="/state/lithuanian-soviet-socialist-republic">Lithuanian Soviet Socialist Republic</a></li></ul></section>
